## Comparing Searched and Unsearched Stops

We compared driver ages and search percentages by recorded race and gender to explore characteristics associated with a search being conducted.

```{r, echo=FALSE, fig.width=7, fig.height=5}
# Compare ages between searched and unsearched stops.
boxplot(driver_age ~ search_conducted,
        data = stops,
        names = c("Not searched", "Searched"),
        main = "Driver Age by Search Status",
        xlab = "Search status",
        ylab = "Driver age (years)",
        col = c("lightblue", "lightgreen"))
```

```{r, include=FALSE}
# Calculate search percentages within each recorded race.
search_by_race <- stops %>%
  filter(!is.na(driver_race), !is.na(search_conducted)) %>%
  group_by(driver_race) %>%
  summarise(
    n_stops = n(),
    search_pct = 100 * mean(search_conducted),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

# Calculate search percentages within each recorded gender.
search_by_gender <- stops %>%
  filter(!is.na(driver_gender), !is.na(search_conducted)) %>%
  group_by(driver_gender) %>%
  summarise(
    n_stops = n(),
    search_pct = 100 * mean(search_conducted),
    .groups = "drop"
  )
```

```{r, echo=FALSE, fig.width=7, fig.height=5}
# Compare percentages rather than raw search counts.
ggplot(search_by_race,
       aes(x = driver_race, y = search_pct)) +
  geom_col(fill = "steelblue") +
  labs(
    title = "Percentage of Stops Involving a Search by Race",
    x = "Recorded driver race",
    y = "Stops involving a search (%)"
  ) +
  theme_classic()
```

```{r, echo=FALSE, fig.width=7, fig.height=5}
ggplot(search_by_gender,
       aes(x = driver_gender, y = search_pct)) +
  geom_col(fill = "steelblue") +
  labs(
    title = "Percentage of Stops Involving a Search by Gender",
    x = "Recorded driver gender",
    y = "Stops involving a search (%)"
  ) +
  theme_classic()
```

Searched drivers were generally younger than unsearched drivers, with median ages of approximately 27 and 36 years, respectively. Search percentages also varied by recorded race: approximately 3.2% of stops involving Black drivers and 3.1% involving Hispanic drivers resulted in searches, compared with 1.3% for White drivers, 0.7% for Other, and 0.4% for Asian drivers. Approximately 2.1% of stops involving male drivers resulted in searches, compared with 0.8% involving female drivers. These unadjusted comparisons describe associations among recorded stops and do not establish causation or discrimination.


## Exploratory Summary

Overall, our exploratory analysis identified differences in search rates and contraband discovery across several driver and traffic-stop characteristics. We addressed missing data, transformed driver age, and examined relationships among predictors to prepare the dataset for logistic regression. Although some differences were observed across racial groups, these unadjusted comparisons do not establish discrimination. Our cleaned dataset provides a foundation for further modeling and evaluation.
